# The runtime's committed Node oracle corpora are tab-separated, hex-encoded
# ASCII generated once by gen-*-cases.mjs (see packages/runtime/test/). They
# are stored LF-only. Without this file, GitHub's Windows runner image ships
# core.autocrlf=true, and a checkout on that runner would silently rewrite
# every line to CRLF -- which materializeHostCases() in
# packages/runtime/test/cc.ts compares byte-for-byte against this host's
# freshly generated (LF-only) output, so EVERY non-cwd-bound case would
# disagree by a trailing \r that GitHub's log viewer does not render. Pin
# these to LF so that risk cannot materialise, on any platform.
#
# HYGIENE, NOT A DIAGNOSIS: this rule was added while investigating a real
# Windows path.test.ts CI failure, on the hypothesis that CRLF corruption
# explained it. That hypothesis was WRONG and is retracted -- the CI log's
# "committed"/"this host" pairs rendered byte-identical because the
# underlying VALUES genuinely differed (a drive-letter cwd dependency in
# path.win32.relative that the cwd probe never sampled), not because of an
# invisible \r. See e53695b5c ("PROBE_CWDS never probed a drive letter
# already in the corpus") for the actual mechanism and fix; CRLF played no
# part in that failure, with or without this file. This rule earns its
# place on its own merits -- it closes a real, independently demonstrated
# checkout-time risk (verified by cloning with -c core.autocrlf=true) -- not
# because it cured anything observed in CI.
packages/runtime/test/*-cases.txt text eol=lf
